WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.278 --> 00:00:03.944
Language assistance is useful when someone can find it,

2
00:00:04.211 --> 00:00:06.944
request it and receive it in time to participate.

3
00:00:07.656 --> 00:00:10.789
In this lesson, we will connect standards five, six

4
00:00:10.811 --> 00:00:13.856
and seven to the steps before, during and after

5
00:00:13.878 --> 00:00:14.522
an encounter.

6
00:00:15.497 --> 00:00:19.630
Standard five addresses timely language assistance without cost to

7
00:00:19.652 --> 00:00:23.030
the individual. Standard six addresses making people aware of

8
00:00:23.052 --> 00:00:26.697
that assistance orally and in writing. Standard seven addresses

9
00:00:26.719 --> 00:00:30.319
the competence of people providing language assistance. The standards

10
00:00:30.341 --> 00:00:33.519
work together: an available service does little good if

11
00:00:33.541 --> 00:00:35.674
someone does not know about it or cannot request

12
00:00:35.697 --> 00:00:36.097
it.

13
00:00:36.974 --> 00:00:40.886
For your specific work, use current agency procedures and

14
00:00:40.908 --> 00:00:44.730
the responsible program's guidance. This lesson does not establish

15
00:00:44.752 --> 00:00:48.663
a new vendor, reimbursement rule, eligibility rule or approval

16
00:00:48.686 --> 00:00:51.730
process. Knowing how to locate the right procedure is

17
00:00:51.752 --> 00:00:52.797
part of being prepared.

18
00:00:54.008 --> 00:00:57.230
Return to the service-planning notice. It gives a date

19
00:00:57.252 --> 00:00:59.519
and a number to call, but does not explain

20
00:00:59.541 --> 00:01:03.741
how to request assistance. A practical review asks whether

21
00:01:03.763 --> 00:01:07.408
the notice makes assistance visible, whether the request method

22
00:01:07.430 --> 00:01:10.874
is usable, and whether someone is responsible for responding.

23
00:01:11.462 --> 00:01:15.840
Ask about preferred language and communication needs respectfully, using

24
00:01:15.862 --> 00:01:19.062
an approved process. Do not assume that a person

25
00:01:19.084 --> 00:01:21.840
wants an interpreter because of their name or accent,

26
00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:24.040
or assume that they do not because they can

27
00:01:24.062 --> 00:01:28.173
hold a brief conversation in English. Understanding everyday conversation

28
00:01:28.196 --> 00:01:31.684
and understanding a complex service decision are different tasks.

29
00:01:32.344 --> 00:01:36.144
When an interpreter is needed, follow the applicable arrangements

30
00:01:36.167 --> 00:01:40.411
early enough to support participation. Confirm the language, any

31
00:01:40.433 --> 00:01:44.344
relevant communication needs, the setting, timing and the information

32
00:01:44.367 --> 00:01:47.656
required for the assignment. Share only information that is

33
00:01:47.678 --> 00:01:51.544
appropriate and necessary through the approved channels. A request

34
00:01:51.567 --> 00:01:54.433
is not complete merely because it was sent; someone

35
00:01:54.456 --> 00:01:56.722
needs to confirm the arrangement and know what to

36
00:01:56.744 --> 00:01:57.544
do if it fails.

37
00:01:58.978 --> 00:02:01.844
Consider a person who arrives with a family member

38
00:02:01.867 --> 00:02:03.978
and says that the family member has been helping

39
00:02:04.000 --> 00:02:08.200
with communication. Treat both people respectfully. Do not assume

40
00:02:08.222 --> 00:02:11.022
that the family member is qualified to interpret complex

41
00:02:11.044 --> 00:02:14.356
information, and do not confuse the person's chosen support

42
00:02:14.378 --> 00:02:18.911
with the organization's responsibility to provide appropriate communication assistance.

43
00:02:19.656 --> 00:02:23.811
The CLAS framework discourages using untrained individuals and minors

44
00:02:23.833 --> 00:02:28.478
as interpreters. Applicable laws and agency procedures also matter.

45
00:02:29.100 --> 00:02:32.256
Follow those requirements and seek the appropriate guidance rather

46
00:02:32.278 --> 00:02:34.922
than making a blanket decision based only on this

47
00:02:34.944 --> 00:02:38.922
example. Explain the available assistance, ask about the person's

48
00:02:38.944 --> 00:02:41.611
preferences, and preserve their involvement in decisions.

49
00:02:42.467 --> 00:02:45.556
A qualified interpreter and a chosen support person can

50
00:02:45.578 --> 00:02:50.178
have different roles. One conveys communication accurately; the other

51
00:02:50.200 --> 00:02:53.711
may provide the support the person wants. Clarifying roles

52
00:02:53.733 --> 00:02:56.089
helps avoid putting someone in a position they are

53
00:02:56.111 --> 00:02:58.222
not prepared or authorized to fill.

54
00:02:59.303 --> 00:03:03.037
Address the person directly. Use clear language and manageable

55
00:03:03.059 --> 00:03:07.414
portions of information. Allow time for interpretation and questions.

56
00:03:07.970 --> 00:03:10.259
Avoid speaking about the person as though they are

57
00:03:10.281 --> 00:03:13.370
not present. Check whether the communication method is working,

58
00:03:13.392 --> 00:03:15.303
and respond if there is a problem.

59
00:03:15.981 --> 00:03:19.803
Checking understanding should not feel like testing or blaming

60
00:03:19.826 --> 00:03:22.981
the person. You can explain that you want to

61
00:03:23.003 --> 00:03:25.626
check whether your explanation of the next step was

62
00:03:25.648 --> 00:03:29.226
clear, then invite them to explain what they understand

63
00:03:29.248 --> 00:03:33.314
will happen. If the explanation was unclear, try again

64
00:03:33.337 --> 00:03:36.114
in a different way. Do not simply repeat the

65
00:03:36.137 --> 00:03:37.737
same wording more loudly.

66
00:03:38.429 --> 00:03:42.362
If assistance is unavailable or unsuitable, follow the approved

67
00:03:42.384 --> 00:03:46.429
contingency and escalation process. Do not improvise with a

68
00:03:46.451 --> 00:03:50.762
child, an unapproved automated tool, or an unqualified bystander

69
00:03:50.784 --> 00:03:54.318
to complete a consequential conversation. Explain what you can

70
00:03:54.340 --> 00:03:57.029
do now and how the communication need will be

71
00:03:57.918 --> 00:04:02.229
addressed. Urgent circumstances require the applicable urgent-response procedure, not

72
00:04:02.251 --> 00:04:03.784
a rule invented by this lesson.

73
00:04:04.884 --> 00:04:07.284
Confirm the next step in a form the person

74
00:04:07.307 --> 00:04:11.573
can use. Record operational information required by the applicable

75
00:04:11.596 --> 00:04:16.507
procedure, using approved systems and appropriate privacy protections. A

76
00:04:16.529 --> 00:04:18.529
learning worksheet is not a case record.

77
00:04:19.162 --> 00:04:22.184
If there was a delay or repeated difficulty arranging

78
00:04:22.207 --> 00:04:26.429
assistance, raise that pattern through the appropriate channel. Individual

79
00:04:26.451 --> 00:04:28.918
staff should not have to solve the same preventable

80
00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:33.362
problem repeatedly without organizational support. That connection will matter

81
00:04:33.384 --> 00:04:36.073
when we discuss leadership, resources and improvement.

82
00:04:37.262 --> 00:04:40.684
In the activity below, decide which next step best

83
00:04:40.707 --> 00:04:43.662
supports a person whose assistance request has not been

84
00:04:43.684 --> 00:04:47.040
confirmed. Look for an answer that checks the arrangement,

85
00:04:47.351 --> 00:04:50.440
communicates with the person and uses the appropriate escalation

86
00:04:50.462 --> 00:04:54.084
route. An answer that simply assumes someone else handled

87
00:04:54.107 --> 00:04:56.062
it leaves the barrier unresolved.

88
00:04:56.918 --> 00:05:00.740
For your work example, identify where assistance is offered,

89
00:05:00.940 --> 00:05:03.984
how it is requested, who responds and how completion

90
00:05:04.007 --> 00:05:06.740
is confirmed. If you do not know one of

91
00:05:06.762 --> 00:05:10.051
those steps, record a question for the responsible area.

92
00:05:10.629 --> 00:05:12.718
Do not enter anyone's personal details.

93
00:05:13.520 --> 00:05:16.076
You now have a way to examine the whole

94
00:05:16.098 --> 00:05:19.209
request, rather than just the presence of an assistance

95
00:05:19.231 --> 00:05:23.453
statement. Next, we will look at the information itself:

96
00:05:23.742 --> 00:05:27.364
whether someone can find the message, access it, understand

97
00:05:27.387 --> 00:05:29.539
it, use it and act on it.
